meta {
  name: 02 Provision student
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/centers/me/students
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "firstName": "Awa",
    "lastName": "Mbarga",
    "email": "{{studentEmail}}",
    "phone": "+237690000000",
    "tier": "START"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.activated: eq false
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("studentId", res.body.id);
  bru.setEnvVar("activationKey", res.body.activationKey);
}

docs {
  Set a fresh `studentEmail` before each run. An address already in use is
  refused, deliberately.

  `activationKey` is returned HERE AND ONLY HERE. Only its hash is stored, so it
  cannot be recovered afterwards — a student who loses it needs a new one from
  `05 Re-issue activation key`. The post-response script captures it for
  `Student Activation / 01`.

  The account has no password yet. The center creates it; only the student sets
  the credential. Confirm the response carries no password field at all.

  ## The tier is required

  `tier` decides what the student may do, so there is no default. A center
  holding several tiers has no obvious one, and quietly defaulting to the
  cheapest would put a student the school meant to give the exam module into a
  tier without it.

  - omit `tier` entirely  -> 400
  - `"tier": "GOLD"`      -> 400
  - `"tier": "start"`     -> 400, the enum is upper case
  - `"tier": "START"`, `"PRO"` or `"PREMIUM"` -> 201

  A fresh center holds one START seat, so START is the only value it can use
  until it buys more.

  ## The seat check is PER TIER

  This is the case worth setting up by hand, because a total-based check would
  pass it wrongly. Give a center one Start seat and five Pro:

      UPDATE center_seats SET quantity = 1 WHERE center_id = '...' AND tier = 'START';
      INSERT INTO center_seats (id, center_id, tier, quantity, unit_price_xaf)
      VALUES (gen_random_uuid()::text, '...', 'PRO', 5, 10000);

  - provision one START student -> 201
  - provision a second START student -> 403 SEAT_LIMIT_REACHED, with
    `tier: "START"` in the body. Five free Pro seats do not help a Start
    student, and the refusal has to say which tier is full — "no seats left"
    is useless to a center holding three.
  - provision a PRO student -> 201, from the same center, immediately.
  - provision a PREMIUM student -> 403 TIER_NOT_HELD, with
    `tier: "PREMIUM"`. A different code from a full tier on purpose: one is
    fixed by buying the tier, the other by buying more seats, and those ask
    the center for different actions.

  ## On a fresh trial center

  One START seat, so the second student is 403 SEAT_LIMIT_REACHED. That
  refusal is the moment to sell, not a bug.

  Checks worth doing by hand:

  - the same email twice          -> 409 STUDENT_EMAIL_ALREADY_EXISTS.
    Attaching would hand this school a stranger existing account.
  - `"password": "hunter2"`       -> 400. A center must never set a password.
  - `"centerId": "other-center"`  -> 400. Ownership comes from the token only.
  - `"activatedAt": "2026-01-01"` -> 400

  Those last three are the security boundary: the DTO allowlist rejects them
  before any handler runs.
}
